--  Putting a computed value inside a sentence.
--
--  This is what 'Image was wanted for. Before it, a program could compute a
--  number and could not say anything *about* it: the value went on a line of
--  its own, under a fixed heading, because there was no way to join it to
--  surrounding text.
--
--  The images are Ada's own. Integer'Image puts a space before a non-negative
--  value -- which looks like a bug in the shell and is not -- and Float'Image
--  writes the long form rather than a friendlier short one. A shorter form
--  would drop digits the number was carrying, and either change would make
--  this program print something different under a real Ada compiler.
--
--  'Image of a String is refused rather than returning the text. Ada 2022 does
--  define it, and what it yields is the text in quotes with the non-graphic
--  characters bracketed -- so returning the text would be the plausible wrong
--  answer. See ROADMAP.md.

Count : Integer := 3;
Ratio : Float := 1.5;
Ready : Boolean := True;
Mark  : Character := '*';

--  The space before 3 is Ada's, so the text either side is written to suit it.
put_line ("found" & Count'Image & " of them");

--  The same thing written as an interpolated string, which is Ada 2022's
--  syntax for it and means exactly the concatenation above.
put_line (f"found{Count'Image} of them");

--  The braces want a String, so a number goes in through 'Image. That is Ada's
--  rule, not a limitation here: f"found {Count} of them" is refused, and says
--  so in terms of the `&` this rewrites to.
put_line (f"ratio is{Ratio'Image}, ready: {Ready'Image}");

--  A literal brace is escaped, since braces are the delimiters.
put_line (f"in braces: \{{Count'Image}\}");

put_line ("ratio is" & Ratio'Image);
put_line ("ready: " & Ready'Image);
put_line ("marker is " & Mark'Image);

--  The prefix is an expression to evaluate, not just a variable to read.
function Doubled (N : Integer) return Integer is
begin
   return N * 2;
end Doubled;

put_line ("doubled:" & Doubled (Count)'Image);

--  Which makes a subprogram able to describe what it was given.
procedure Report (Label : String; Value : Integer) is
begin
   put_line (Label & " =" & Value'Image);
end Report;

Report ("width", 80);
Report ("height", 24);

--  And an out parameter can be described from inside the body that fills it.
procedure Measure (Total : out Integer) is
begin
   Total := 0;

   for Step in 1 .. 4 loop
      Total := Total + Step;
   end loop;

   put_line ("measured" & Total'Image);
end Measure;

Sum : Integer := 0;
Measure (Sum);
put_line ("caller sees" & Sum'Image);

--  And back the other way. `Integer'Value` reads a number out of text, which is
--  what a program that has just read a line needs before it can do anything but
--  compare it: reading gives you a String, and arithmetic does not.
Counted : Integer := Integer'Value ("42");

put_line (f"read back{Counted'Image} and one more is{Integer'Image (Counted + 1)}");

--  `Integer'Image (N)` is how Ada has always written it, and `N'Image` is the
--  same thing said the other way round. Both are here because refusing one
--  while accepting the other would be accepting half of a thing.
Same : Boolean := Integer'Image (Counted) = Counted'Image;

--  Text that does not hold a number raises, as it does in Ada. A shell that
--  answered zero would be inventing a number nobody wrote.

--  Where a value sits in its type, and how to get back from there.
--
--  `'Pos` and `'Val` are the only way to reach a character code. Without them
--  a script could not write a tab outside an interpolated literal, could not
--  compare against a byte value, and could not step from one letter to the
--  next.
put_line ("A is at " & Trim (Character'Pos ('A')'Image));
put_line ("and 66 is " & Character'Val (66));

--  `'Succ` and `'Pred` are a position, an addition, and a position back --
--  which is what Ada defines them as, so they cannot disagree with the two
--  above. Going past the last value raises rather than wrapping.
put_line ("after a comes " & Character'Succ ('a'));

--  An Integer is its own position, so both directions are the value itself.
put_line ("7 is at " & Trim (Integer'Pos (7)'Image));

--  A type's own bounds, which take no argument. What a script guarding an
--  addition or clamping a value has to be able to name.
put_line ("the largest Integer is " & Trim (Integer'Last'Image));
put_line ("the last Character is at "
          & Trim (Character'Pos (Character'Last)'Image));
